<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurasia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eurasian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurasia is the combined landmass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Its steppe belt and trade routes linked empires across vast distances, enabling the expansion of nomadic powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, and the exchange of goods and ideas through empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurasia.jpg" alt="Eurasia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keepscases · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurasia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
